{"name":"Hellebore","hex":"#646944","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hellebore","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the common garden flowering plants of the genus Helleborus, in family Ranunculaceae, having supposed medicinal properties.","A toxic extract of certain false hellebores (Veratrum album or Veratrum viride), formerly used as a pesticide."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hellebore"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hellebore","blocks":[{"text":"Commonly known as hellebores, the Eurasian genus Helleborus consists of approximately 20 species of herbaceous or evergreen perennial flowering plants in the family Ranunculaceae, within which it gave its name to the tribe of Helleboreae. Many hellebore species are poisonous."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Illustration_Helleborus_niger0.jpg/330px-Illustration_Helleborus_niger0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hellebore","wikidata":"Q147289"},{"title":"Hellebore (magazine)","blocks":[{"text":"Hellebore is a small press magazine devoted to British folk horror and the occult. It was a World Fantasy Awards finalist in 2022."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Hellbore_Magazine_Issue_2_cover.jpg/330px-Hellbore_Magazine_Issue_2_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hellebore_(magazine)","wikidata":"Q118903712"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Helleborus_foetidus_re.jpg/500px-Helleborus_foetidus_re.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Helleborus_foetidus_re.jpg/1280px-Helleborus_foetidus_re.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/